INT. WEDDING-CAKE BAKERY - DAWN

Marble counters, copper pans, a window steamed with flour dust. The first light turns every tier of sugared lace to pale gold.

ODETTE LAMBERT-OKONKWO hunches over a six-tier cake, a piping bag trembling in her fist. She is thirty-four and has not slept. She works with the stubborn precision of someone refusing to feel anything.

JULIAN VESPER enters through the back door, shaking rain from his coat. He is tall, forty, rumpled in an expensive way. He stops when he sees her. He had meant to be quiet.

She does not turn. She knows his footsteps.

CECILE LAMBERT sits on a stool by the oven, ninety years old, wrapped in a shawl. She has watched this kitchen for sixty years. She stirs her tea and says nothing.

Hamid, the apprentice, HAMID BAJWA (20s, apron, headphones), kneads dough at the far counter, oblivious. Beside the cooling racks, TOBIAS REEVE (50s, groom's father, rumpled tuxedo, uninvited at this hour) rubs his eyes, a cup of coffee in each hand.

ODETTE
You're early.

JULIAN
You're awake.

ODETTE
It's a wedding. I'm always awake.

He crosses the room. She keeps piping. A tiny sugar rose blooms under her hand, then another. He watches. His fingers hover near her shoulder, then fall away.

JULIAN
It's beautiful.

ODETTE
It's a cake.

JULIAN
It's beautiful, and I'm sorry.

She sets the bag down. Her hands are shaking now, openly.

ODETTE
Julian, not today.

JULIAN
When, then?

ODETTE
Not today. This is somebody else's day.

Cecile sets her cup in its saucer. The clink is quiet and exact.

CECILE
(not looking up)
Child, let him speak.

ODETTE
Grand-mère.

CECILE
Let him speak.

Julian takes a breath. Beside him, Tobias lowers both cups and begins to back away toward the door.

JULIAN
I should have come back the first week. I should have called. I should have done a hundred things.

ODETTE
You should have.

JULIAN
But I didn't, because I was ashamed.

She turns at last. Her eyes are red, her mouth a straight line. A smear of white frosting marks her cheek like a scar.

ODETTE
Ashamed of what?

JULIAN
That you were better than me.

The oven timer shrieks. Hamid pulls his headphones down, blinks at the room, and freezes. Nobody moves to silence the timer.

ODETTE
(low)
You walked out of my kitchen in the middle of service.

JULIAN
I know.

ODETTE
Eight years. And you come back on the morning of someone else's wedding.

JULIAN
I came back because I could not stand a single day more.

Her hand closes around the piping bag. Frosting oozes between her fingers onto the marble.

ODETTE
Get out.

JULIAN
Odette--

ODETTE
Get out!

She hurls the bag. It strikes the wall behind him, bursting in a starburst of buttercream. Julian does not flinch. He steps closer. Tobias bolts for the door, spilling coffee.

Cecile rises with great effort, shawl trailing, and shuffles to the timer. She switches it off. Silence crashes down.

CECILE
(quietly)
Now. Both of you. Finish the cake.
